hey guys so back in winter we told you  guys we would give you a rundown on what  makes a scarlet a special camera so  finally uh seven months afterwards  we've gotten around to doing so so the  scarlet is the uh the very first really  professional camera that we've owned  it's kind of like a professional  filmmaker's dream come true for us  because it's a true cinematic camera we  could go out and shoot something with  this  that is of the quality that you can go  into the theaters and see and don't get  me wrong like the 7d is great camera the  sony fs100 like all those cameras  great cameras but generally speaking you  couldn't go to a theater um and shoot  with one of those and have it be in the  cinema all right so one of the most  notable things about the red camera is  that it shoots raw now some of you  photographers out there you know are  familiar with the format but most of you  guys shoot video so the way your camera  works is you have a digital sensor light  comes in hits at sensor that sensor  turns that light into electrical ones  and zeros sends them off to your  camera's processor your camera takes  those signals and turns them into an  image and now what you finally get on  your little memory card at the end of  the day is that finished image encoded  as h.264 mpeg2 whatever the format is  that your camera is shooting in now what  that also means all the things are  happening after the light hits the  sensor  that's all baked in for good you can't  go back and change that but when you're  shooting in raw with this camera you can  go back you can change things like your  white balance you can go change your iso  after the fact you can go change your  saturation your color balance basically  anything that is done after the light  hits the sensor the only things  that you can't adjust are the things  from before the light hits the sensor  are you in focus your shutter speed your  aperture  those three things are basically the  only things you have to worry about with  this camera it makes things much quicker  and easier to shoot you're rolling  running in a car and a cloud goes with  the sun and suddenly the temperature  changes of the light you don't have to  worry about it you can fix it later so  for us that's a big thing so we got a  small crew and we run a gun so another  special thing about this camera is that  it shoots in 4k resolution now what is a  k 4k 2k what does that all mean  basically a k is the horizontal  resolution your image for example 1080p  is roughly 2k it's 1920 pixels aka 1.9 k  or 2k if you round up this camera shoots  4k generally most of the stuff we shoot  is at a resolution of 3840  by 2160 so you got a lot of pixels to  play with that means if we need to  stabilize the shot afterwards and it's  cropped in order to pull a close-up from  a medium shot we can do it with this  camera and you guys can't really notice  the difference on youtube because they  have so much extra resolution to play  with  everything looks better downscaled and  this camera gives us a chance to do that  so 4k resolution we thought we would  never use it but guess what it's super  convenient  so one of the things that we as  filmmakers and probably all you guys on  the internet have sought after for all  these years is the film look what is the  film look at first everybody thought  that was 24 frames per second so all the  cameras got 24 frames per second and  that helped but that wasn't the film  look shallow depth of field lenses that  went a long ways and now you have things  like dslrs that let you shoot video with  those nice lenses in the big format  sensors that gives you that really  cinematic shallow depth of field and  that's close but that's still not the  film look the film look  comes from high dynamic range what is  that  well basically your camera  the sensor on your camera has a range of  light that it can take in there's a  certain point where the light gets too  dark and it goes to black basically it  turns into noise you can boost it but  you're just gonna get snow  and then you have another point at which  the sensor clips when it gets too much  light and you just get white so the  range between the part where your sensor  clips and when it's just black noise  that's your dynamic range some cameras  like your iphone only have a couple  stops of dynamic range if you go outside  you take a picture in the sunlight  the shadow side of your face is going to  be black while the sunny side is exposed  or if you expose the shadow side the  sunny sides can be blown out white but  if you use a better camera you can  actually see the detail in the shadow  and you can see the detail on the bright  part so for example this camera if i  film a shot here looking out the garage  i can actually get all these stuff  outside of the garage in direct sunlight  to expose without clipping and i can get  the stuff inside the garage to expose  without descending into noise he tried  to do it with another camera like an  iphone or even the fs100 you can't get  the same amount of range now the thing  is your eyes as a human being have way  more stops and dynamic range than any  camera so the more a camera can mimic  that the more vibrant and alive and  painterly an image becomes so another  thing that makes this camera exceptional  is that it's built to be modular you  might notice the camera body itself is  essentially just a box and we have a  bunch of things added onto it got a  battery plate back here for putting  bigger batteries on it i got handles  that i've added to the top that are  bolted into the top  i've got a side handle that bolts onto  the side and of course i have a lens on  the front you can actually take all this  stuff off and rearrange it however you  want rather than the camera according to  a cf card or sd card it actually records  to solid-state drives known as a red meg  basically it's just a solid-state drive  inside of this a little expensive but  they're also very fast and very durable  so you can offload 64 gigabytes of  footage in like 10 minutes shooting  gigantic 4k file sizes raw  how big of a file are you dealing with  at the end of the day  relatively speaking the files are about  one and a half to 1.2 gigs per minute  which is not too bad actually every  video since footloose you have shot with  a scarlet including footloose there's  something cathartic in making a video as  widely downvoted and ridiculous as  footloose and shooting that on a red  camera  but yeah basically uh tether um art of  the insta kill  uh photo reel footloose etc we're all  shot with this camera we have a workflow  figured out that actually works really  well a lot of people actually  wonder how you can work with these new  files and keep it as fast as you need to  be when you're on youtube it actually  works great for us we turn it around  just as fast as would in any other case  also some of you guys are probably  saying  well i don't have a scarlet how can i  make my stuff look good or i don't need  a scarlet my stuff looks great you're  right you don't need a scarlet you can  make your stuff look great the whole  point with this camera is that it makes  your life easier it makes it easier to  get a pretty image it's not to say you  can't get a pretty image with another  camera it's a great tool it can handle  things in a way that makes it so we can  shoot faster we can shoot quicker and  get a better looking result with less  work that's the value of this camera we  would not be able to have this camera  without you guys support thank you very  much for watching our videos and sharing  it and all that kind of stuff it has  helped us achieve our cinematic dream of  being filmmakers with big boy filmmaker  tools so  thanks guys and uh  enjoy watching the videos  you
 

